From ddd3759f5d89935b3f1e17934d2b60ca1dca95e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gli_acopo <82150202+GliAcopo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:19:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] embed videos in exported PDF files

A <video> (an animated gif) was exported to PDF as the picture of its
current frame only.  The animation box of a <video> now tells the renderer
that it displays the video (renderer_rep::embed_video, ignored by the
other renderers), and the PDF renderer adds a RichMedia annotation over
the picture, as the media9 LaTeX package does.

The embedded MP4 video is made from the image of the document only, with
the frames as TeXmacs shows them: coalesced by ImageMagick as in
decompose_gif, on a white background, each lasting the duration given in
the <video> tag (ffmpeg; libx264, or mpeg4 when ffmpeg lacks libx264;
without metadata, so that exports are reproducible).  An animated gif
cannot be embedded as such: PDF viewers do not play it (VLC, used by
Okular, opens it as a still image), and gif delays are multiples of 10 ms.
Each file is embedded once, and the catalog declares PDF 1.7 with Adobe
extension level 3, which RichMedia requires.

In Okular the video starts when its page becomes visible, loops when the
animation repeats, and a click pauses it.  Viewers without video support
show the picture, as before.  Without ImageMagick or ffmpeg the export is
unchanged, with a warning.
